 ## Overview
-Lit sphere shading is a non-photorealistic illumination technique that captures artistic lighting styles by using an image of a sphere, shaded in a desired style, as a lookup table [S1][S2, unofficial]. It represents the shading of an object as a function of eye-space normal orientation, allowing the sphere to act as a proxy object for illumination [S1][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Volume Illustration Tone Shading](805-volume-illustration-tone-shading.md) and [Vignette Shading](1830-vignette-shading.md) by relying on a geometric sphere proxy rather than scalar field properties or screen-space effects, and from [Blinn-Phong Toon Shading](4708-blinn-phong-toon-shading.md) by encoding pre-computed artistic styles rather than computing analytical geometric light interactions [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Lit sphere shading is a non-photorealistic rendering technique that captures artistic lighting styles by using an image of a sphere shaded in a desired style as a lookup table [S1][S2, unofficial]. It represents the shading of an object as a function of eye-space normal orientation, allowing the sphere to act as a proxy object for illumination [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike photometric models that calculate light transport, this method maps normal vectors directly to pre-computed artistic colors, providing view-dependent lighting that rotates with the camera [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
